#os.system() returns the exit status of the command executed. A status code of 0 typically indicates that the command was executed successfully, while a non-zero status code indicates an error or failure in executing the command.
#In the example above, the os.system() function is used to execute the "ping -n 1 google.com" command, which sends a single ping request to google.com. The status code returned by os.system() is then checked to determine if the ping was successful or not. If the status code is 0, it indicates that the ping was successful; otherwise, it indicates that the ping failed.
#The subprocess.call() function is used to execute a command in a subprocess and returns the exit status of the command. Similar to os.system(), a status code of 0 indicates that the command was executed successfully, while a non-zero status code indicates an error or failure in executing the command.
#In the example above, the subprocess.call() function is used to execute the "ping -n 1 google.com" command, which sends a single ping request to google.com. The status code returned by subprocess.call() is then checked to determine if the ping was successful or not. If the status code is 0, it indicates that the ping was successful; otherwise, it indicates that the ping failed.
status_code = os.system("ping -n 1 google.com")
if status_code == 0:
    print("Ping successful.")
else:
    print("Ping failed.")
    
status_code = subprocess.call(["ping", "-n", "1", "google.com"])
if status_code == 0:
    print("Ping successful.")
    



